Step 1: Check Docker Installation
Open Command Prompt or Terminal and enter:
docker --version
If Docker is installed correctly, its version will be displayed.
Example:
Docker version 28.x.x
Step 2: Start Docker Desktop
Open Docker Desktop and make sure Docker Engine is running.
Docker must be running before executing Docker commands.
Step 3: Login to Docker Hub
Open the terminal and run:
docker login
Enter your Docker Hub username and password when prompted.
If the login is successful, Docker will display a successful login message.
Step 4: Select a Docker Hub Repository
Use a Docker Hub image that has already been uploaded.
For example:
yourusername/student-management-api:latest
Here:
· yourusername is the Docker Hub username.
· student-management-api is the repository name.
· latest is the image tag.
Replace yourusername with the actual Docker Hub username.
Step 5: Pull the Docker Image
Run the following command:
docker pull yourusername/student-management-api:latest
Docker will download the image from the Docker Hub repository to the local computer.
Example:
docker pull rahul123/student-management-api:latest
After the download is completed, Docker will display the downloaded image layers.
Step 6: Verify the Docker Image
To view the downloaded Docker images, run:
docker images
Expected output:
REPOSITORY TAG IMAGE ID CREATED
yourusername/student-management-api latest abc123456789 ...
This confirms that the Docker image has been successfully downloaded to the local system.
Step 7: Run the Docker Image
Run the downloaded image using:
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name student-api yourusername/student-managementapi:latest
Example:
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name student-api rahul123/student-management-api:latest
Here:
-d runs the container in detached mode.
-p 5000:5000 maps the local port 5000 to the container port 5000.
--name student-api assigns a name to the container.
Step 8: Check the Running Container
Run:
docker ps
